---
import PageLayout from "../../../layouts/PageLayout.astro";
import HeartButton from "../../../components/demos/HeartButton.astro";
import ToggleSwitch from "../../../components/demos/ToggleSwitch.astro";
---

<PageLayout
  title="Lab | Optimistic vs Pessimistic UI"
  description="A decision guide with interactive examples showing when to use optimistic vs pessimistic UI updates."
>
  <article class="page case-study">
    <a class="back-link" href="/lab"><- Back to lab</a>

    <header class="page-hero case-study__header">
      <p class="eyebrow">Lab / UI Patterns</p>
      <h1>Optimistic vs Pessimistic UI</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Both patterns solve the same problem&mdash;how does the UI respond after
        a user action while waiting for the server? The choice depends on what
        you&rsquo;re willing to be wrong about.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose guide-body">
      <section>
        <h2>What it is</h2>
        <p>
          Two strategies for handling the gap between a user action and the
          server response:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Pessimistic</strong> &mdash; wait for the server to confirm
            before updating the UI. The user sees a loading state until the
            server responds.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Optimistic</strong> &mdash; update the UI immediately as if
            the action succeeded, then reconcile with the server in the
            background. If the server fails, roll back.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>How it works</h2>
        <p>
          The difference is just a single step reordered. Both patterns send the
          same request and take the same time. The only question is
          <strong>when</strong> the UI reflects the result.
        </p>

        <h3>Pessimistic flow</h3>
        <pre><code>User clicks &#x2192; show loading &#x2192; server responds &#x2192; update UI &#x2192; done</code></pre>
        <p>
          Every step is sequential. The UI never shows a state that hasn&rsquo;t
          been confirmed by the server. Safe, but the user waits on every action.
        </p>

        <h3>Optimistic flow</h3>
        <pre><code>User clicks &#x2192; update UI &#x2192; server responds &#x2192; reconcile &#x2192; done</code></pre>
        <p>
          The UI updates on click, then the server call happens in parallel. If
          the server succeeds, nothing changes. If it fails, the UI rolls back
          and shows an error.
        </p>

        <h3>The tradeoff</h3>
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th></th>
              <th>Pessimistic</th>
              <th>Optimistic</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Perceived speed</td>
              <td>Slow &mdash; user waits for server</td>
              <td>Instant &mdash; feels responsive</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Correctness on error</td>
              <td>Always correct &mdash; never lies</td>
              <td>Must roll back on failure</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Cost of being wrong</td>
              <td>Low &mdash; user sees a spinner</td>
              <td>Higher &mdash; rollback can confuse users</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Complexity</td>
              <td>Trivial</td>
              <td>Low to moderate &mdash; need undo logic</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>See it in action</h2>
        <p>
          Tap each heart below. Watch how the <strong>UI</strong> and the
          <strong>server panel</strong> behave differently under each strategy.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>

    <aside class="guide-demo" aria-label="Interactive demo: heart button comparison">
      <HeartButton />
    </aside>

    <div class="prose guide-body">
      <section>
        <h3>What to notice</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Pessimistic:</strong> The heart stays empty while the server
            processes. The status shows a spinner. Only after the server responds
            (201 Created) does the heart fill. The user waits the full round trip.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Optimistic:</strong> The heart fills instantly. The server
            panel still shows the request in flight, but the UI already reflects
            the new state. The user moves on while the server catches up.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Example: a settings toggle</h2>
        <p>
          Two identical settings toggles below. Both connect to the same mocked
          server and take the same time to respond. The only difference is the
          UI strategy.
        </p>
        <p>
          Click each one and watch how they handle the delay.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>

    <aside class="guide-demo" aria-label="Interactive demo: toggle switch comparison">
      <ToggleSwitch />
    </aside>

    <div class="prose guide-body">
      <section>
        <h3>What to notice</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Pessimistic:</strong> The toggle stays put while the request
            is in flight. The knob pulses to show loading. After ~1.2&thinsp;s
            the switch moves. The user waits.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Optimistic:</strong> The toggle slides immediately on click.
            The server still takes ~1.2&thinsp;s, but the UI already reflects
            the new state. The user moves on.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Should you always be optimistic?</h3>
        <p>No. A settings toggle is a safe case for optimism because:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>The action is trivial to undo</li>
          <li>The user can&rsquo;t act on wrong data (it&rsquo;s just a toggle)</li>
          <li>Failure is rare and rollback is simple</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          But for actions like submitting a payment or changing an email address,
          pessimism is safer &mdash; the user needs to know the action actually
          succeeded before moving on.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>More examples coming soon</h2>
        <p>
          This guide will grow to cover delete actions, payment forms, chat
          messages, drag-and-drop reordering, and settings changes &mdash; each
          with its own interactive demo and reasoning.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </article>
</PageLayout>

<style>
  .guide-demo {
    max-width: 42rem;
    margin: var(--space-gap-2xl) auto;
    padding: var(--space-gap-xl);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-surface) 50%, transparent);
    box-shadow: var(--shadow-sm);
  }
</style>
